lits the column into two non‑empty parts:  \n\n  \\[\n  \\underbrace{(1,j),\\dots ,(j-1,j)}_{\\text{upper part}},\\qquad\n  \\underbrace{(j+1,j),\\dots ,(n,j)}_{\\text{lower part}} .\n  \\]\n\n  Cover the upper part with a vertical tile of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\) and the lower part\n  with a vertical tile of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).\n\nAll these tiles are disjoint, each lies on grid lines, and together they cover every\nsquare that is not on the diagonal.  \nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{column }1)+1\\;(\\text{column }n)+\\sum_{j=2}^{n-1}2\n   =2+2(n-2)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nConsider any admissible tiling (for an arbitrary permutation \\(\\pi\\)).  \nFix a column \\(j\\) and let the uncovered cell in this column be \\((r_j,j)\\) where\n\\(r_j=\\pi^{-1}(j)\\).\n\n*If \\(r_j=1\\) or \\(r_j=n\\)* (the uncovered cell is at the top or bottom of the column), the remaining squares of column \\(j\\) form a single contiguous vertical segment; they can be covered by at most one tile, and at least one tile is required.\n\n*If \\(1<r_j<n\\)*, the uncovered cell splits column \\(j\\) into two non‑empty vertical\nsegments:\n\n\\[\nS^{\\text{up}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i< r_j\\},\\qquad\nS^{\\text{down}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j< i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA tile cannot contain the uncovered cell \\((r_j,j)\\); therefore the two segments\nmust belong to **different** tiles. Consequently column \\(j\\) needs **at least two**\ntiles.\n\nThus every column contributes at least one tile, and each of the \\(n-2\\) interior\ncolumns (those whose uncovered cell is not at an endpoint) contributes at least a\nsecond tile. Hence any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{first column})+1\\;(\\text{last column})+2\\,(n-2)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n-----------------------------------------