1empty vertical segments  \n     \\[\n     U_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i< r_j\\},\\qquad\n     L_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j< i\\le n\\}.\n     \\]\n     Because a tile cannot contain the uncovered cell, the two segments must belong to **different** tiles. Hence column \\(j\\) requires **at least two** tiles.\n\n   Consequently every column contributes at least one tile, and each of the \\(n-2\\) interior columns (those whose uncovered cell is not at an endpoint) contributes a second, distinct tile. Therefore any admissible tiling needs at least  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\text{first column})+1\\;(\\text{last column})+2\\,(n-2)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n   *Why the tiles counted for different columns cannot be the same.*  \n   A single rectangular tile intersects a given column in a **contiguous** set of rows. If a column needs two distinct tiles (as in the interior case), those two tiles intersect the column in two disjoint row intervals, one lying entirely above the uncovered cell and the other entirely below it. No rectangle can intersect the same column in two disjoint intervals; therefore the two tiles required for column \\(j\\) are distinct from the tiles required for any other column. Hence the counting above is valid.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction of step\u202f2 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and step\u202f3 shows that no tiling can use fewer. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe have an \\(n\\times n\\) board of unit squares.  \nA placement of axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles must satisfy:\n\n* each tile\u2019s sides lie on grid lines,\n* no two tiles overlap (each unit square is covered by at most one tile),\n* in every row and in every column there is **exactly one** unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nDenote by \\((i,c_i)\\) the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\n\\pi :\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \\pi(i)=c_i\n\\]\n\nis a bijection; i.e. \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation.  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na permutation matrix.\n\nAll other \\(n^{2}-n=n(n-1)\\) squares must be partitioned into axis\u2011parallel rectangles (the tiles).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n* **Column\u202f1.** The uncovered cell is \\((1,1)\\).  \n  The cells \\((2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) form a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-1)\\times1\\).  \n  Place one tile covering exactly these squares.\n\n* **Column\u202f\\(n\\).** The uncovered cell is \\((n,n)\\).  \n  The cells \\((1,n),\\dots ,(n-1,n)\\) form a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-1)\\times1\\).  \n  Place one tile covering exactly these squares.\n\n* **Column\u202f\\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-1\\).**  \n  The uncovered cell \\((j,j)\\) splits the column into two non\u2011empty parts:  \n\n  \\[\n  U_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\},\\qquad\n  L_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Cover \\(U_j\\) with a vertical tile of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\) and cover \\(L_j\\) with a vertical tile of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).\n\nAll tiles are disjoint, each lies on grid lines, and together they cover every square that is not on the diagonal.  \nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{col }1)+1\\;(\\text{col }n)+\\sum_{j=2}^{n-1}2\n   =2+2(n-2)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nConsider any admissible tiling (for an arbitrary permutation \\(\\pi\\)).  \nFix a column \\(j\\) and let the uncovered cell in that column be \\((r_j,j)\\) where  \n\n\\[\nr_j=\\pi^{-1}(j)\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe column is divided by the uncovered cell into the two (possibly empty) vertical intervals  \n\n\\[\nU_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i< r_j\\},\\qquad\nL_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j< i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile intersects column \\(j\\) in a **contiguous** set of rows; therefore a single tile can lie either completely inside \\(U_j\\), completely inside \\(L_j\\), or contain the uncovered cell (which is forbidden).\n\n*If \\(r_j=1\\) or \\(r_j=n\\).*  \nThen one of the intervals \\(U_j,L_j\\) is empty and the other consists of a single contiguous block of squares. All those squares must be covered, and they cannot be covered together with any square from a different column (otherwise the rectangle would also contain the uncovered cell). Consequently **at least one** tile is required for column \\(j\\).\n\n*If \\(1<r_j<n\\).*  \nBoth \\(U_j\\) and \\(L_j\\) are non\u2011empty. Since a tile cannot contain the uncovered cell, the squares of \\(U_j\\) and those of \\(L_j\\) must belong to **different** tiles. Hence **at least two** tiles are required for column \\(j\\).\n\nThus every column contributes at least one tile, and each of the \\(n-2\\) interior columns (those with \\(1<r_j<n\\)) contributes a second, distinct tile.  \n\nBecause a single rectangle cannot intersect a column in two disjoint intervals, the two tiles required for an interior column are distinct from the tiles required for any other column. Therefore the total number of tiles in any admissible placement is at least  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{first column})+1\\;(\\text{last column})+2\\,(n-2)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction of Section\u202f2 achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and Section\u202f3 proves that no placement can use fewer. Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the problem\u2019s board, \\(n=2025\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 } .\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda can meet the requirement with 4048 rectangular tiles, and it is impossible to do so with fewer."
