nRow1: uncovered col4, squares to cover: col1,2,3.\nRow2: uncovered col1, squares: col2,3,4.\nRow3: uncovered col2, squares: col1,3,4.\nRow4: uncovered col3, squares: col1,2,4.\n\nWe need to cover 12 squares.\n\nPotential large rectangles: maybe rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 2-3 (2x2 block) includes squares (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3). None are uncovered (uncovered at (1,4),(2,1),(3,2),(4,3)). So that's one tile.\n\nAnother rectangle covering rows 3-4, columns 1-2 (2x2 block) includes (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2). But (3,2) is uncovered! So cannot.\n\nMaybe rectangle covering rows 3-4, columns 4 (vertical tile) covering (3,4),(4,4). Both are not uncovered (uncovered at (4,3)). So that's a tile covering 2 squares.\n\nNow remaining squares: (1,1),(1,2? Already covered? Actually (1,2) covered by first tile, (1,3) covered, (1,4) uncovered, (2,1) uncovered, (2,2) covered, (2,3) covered, (2,4) uncovered? Wait (2,4) is not uncovered; uncovered at (2,1). So (2,4) is uncovered? Actually (2,4) is not uncovered, it's a square to be covered. So (2,4) is uncovered? Let's check: uncovered squares are (1,4),(2,1),(3,2),(4,3). So (2,4)",
