e each column except first and last yields two tiles, first and last yield one each). So that's a valid construction.\n\nThe lower bound argument: For each column, the uncovered cell splits column into two intervals. If uncovered cell is at top or bottom (r_j = 1 or n), then one interval is empty and the other is contiguous; they claim at least one tile is required for that column. If uncovered cell is interior (1 < r_j < n), then both intervals are non-empty, and they claim at least two tiles are required for that column. Then they sum up: first column and last column each require at least one tile; interior columns each require at least two distinct tiles. They also claim that the two tiles required for an interior column are distinct from tiles required for any other column, because a rectangle cannot intersect a column in two disjoint intervals. So total tiles >= 2n-2.\n\nWe need to verify that this lower bound argument is valid. Is there any possibility that a tile could cover squares from multiple columns, thereby reducing total tile count? The argument says that each column's intervals must be covered by distinct tiles, but those tiles could also extend horizontally to cover squares in other columns. However, the argument claims that the tiles required for each column are distinct from those required for any other column because a rectangle cannot intersect a column in two disjoint intervals. But that does not preclude a tile covering intervals in multiple columns simultaneously: e.g., a tile could cover the upper interval of column j and also the upper interval of column k in the same rectangle, if those intervals line up horizontally. But the argument seems to assume that each interval must be covered by a distinct tile, but that tile could be the same tile that also covers intervals in other columns. However, the lower bound argument counts each interval as a separate tile, but maybe those intervals could be combined into a single tile that spans multiple columns, thus reducing tile count. Let's examine.\n\nWe have a grid of unit squares. Tiles are axis-aligned rectangles covering contiguous blocks of squares. The uncovered squares are at positions (i, pi(i)). For each column j, the uncovered square is at row r_j. The column is split into two intervals: rows 1..r_j-1 (U_j) and rows r_j+1..n (L_j). The solution says a tile cannot contain the uncovered cell, so any tile covering squares in column j must be entirely within U_j or L_j. So for each column j, the squares in U_j must be covered by some set of tiles, each tile being a rectangle that lies entirely within the region of rows <= r_j-1 in column j. Similarly for L_j.\n\nNow, the lower bound argument says that at least one tile is needed for each interval (if non-empty). But could a single tile cover multiple intervals across columns? For example, consider the upper intervals of columns j and k: they are sets of squares in rows 1..r_j-1 for column j, and rows 1..r_k-1 for column k. A rectangle covering squares in both columns j and k must have rows that are contiguous and columns that are contiguous. So if we want to cover the upper intervals of columns j and k with a single rectangle, that rectangle would need to include all rows from 1 to max(r_j-1, r_k-1) (or maybe up to the minimum?), and columns from j to k. But if r_j and r_k differ, the rectangle might include squares in column j that are below the uncovered cell (if r_j < r_k). That would be a problem because the rectangle cannot include the uncovered cell in column j (which is at row r_j). If the rectangle includes rows > r_j, it would include the uncovered cell (r_j, j) which is not allowed. So the rectangle cannot extend beyond row r_j-1 in column j. Similarly for column k, cannot extend beyond row r_k-1. So the rectangle's top part (upper interval) can be at most up to the minimum of the two r_j-1 values, to avoid covering uncovered cells. But then the rectangle would not cover the rows between that minimum and the larger r_j-1 for the column with larger r_j. So it cannot cover the entire upper interval of that column. So a rectangle cannot simultaneously cover the entire upper interval of two columns if their uncovered rows differ. However, perhaps it could cover part of each interval, but then you'd need additional tiles to cover the remaining parts. The lower bound argument counts at least one tile per column interval, but perhaps you could cover multiple intervals with a single tile if the intervals align perfectly (i.e., all r_j are equal). But the permutation could be such that all uncovered squares are on the same row? That's impossible because each column must have exactly one uncovered square, and each row must have exactly one uncovered square. So the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square. So the r_j values are a permutation of rows. So they are all distinct. So for any two columns j and k, r_j != r_k. So the upper intervals U_j and U_k have different lengths. So a rectangle cannot cover both entire intervals because of the uncovered cell constraints. However, could a rectangle cover a part of U_j and a part of U_k, and the rest of each interval be covered by other tiles? That would still require at least one tile per column interval, but perhaps some intervals could be partially covered by a tile that also covers part of another interval, reducing total tile count? The lower bound argument counts each interval as requiring at least one tile, but does not consider that a tile could cover multiple intervals partially, reducing the total number of tiles needed to cover all intervals. However, the argument also says that each column contributes at least one tile, and each interior column contributes a second distinct tile. But they also claim \"the two tiles required for an interior column are distinct from the tiles required for any other column.\" This is a stronger claim: they assert that 