\dots ,n\\) after relabelling).  \n   By the induction hypothesis the reduced board needs at least \\(2(n-1)-2\\) tiles.  \n   Adding the \\(k-1\\) left\u2011tiles and the \\(n-k\\) right\u2011tiles we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (k-1)+(n-k)+\\bigl[2(n-1)-2\\bigr]=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **any** admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound** \u2013  \n   Choose the *identity* permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n\n   - For each column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) place one vertical tile covering the cells \\((c+1,c),\\,(c+2,c),\\dots ,(n,c)\\).  \n     These \\(n-1\\) tiles cover all cells of \\(L\\) (the region strictly below the diagonal).  \n\n   - For each row \\(r\\;(1\\le r\\le n-1)\\) place one horizontal tile covering the cells \\((r,r+1),\\,(r,r+2),\\dots ,(r,n)\\).  \n     These \\(n-1\\) tiles cover all cells of \\(R\\) (the region strictly above the diagonal).\n\n   The two families are disjoint, they never contain a diagonal cell, and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal unit square.  \n   The total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same value, so the minimum possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles is required to satisfy:\n\n* each tile is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines,\n* no two tiles overlap,\n* each unit square is covered by at most one tile,\n* in every row and in every column there is **exactly one** uncovered unit square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains precisely one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nThe condition \u201cexactly one uncovered square in each column\u2019\u2019 forces \\(\\pi\\) to be a\nbijection; hence \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j):j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered cell belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); a cell cannot belong to both.\nIf a rectangle contained a cell of \\(L\\) and a cell of \\(R\\), then it would also contain\n\\((i,\\pi(i))\\) for the row of the left\u2011hand cell, contradicting the rule that uncovered\nsquares are not covered. Consequently **each tile lies entirely inside \\(L\\) or entirely inside \\(R\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\nWe prove that any tiling of the board satisfying the conditions uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ntiles.  \nThe proof is by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n*Base \\(n=1\\).*  \nThe only cell is uncovered, so no tile is needed; (1) gives \\(0\\), which is correct.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for size \\(n-1\\) and consider a board of size \\(n\\).\nLet the uncovered square in the first row be at column \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n\\));\nthus \\(\\pi(1)=k\\).\n\n**Left side.**  \nThe cells \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,k-1)\\) belong to \\(L\\).  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le k-1\\) the cell \\((1,c)\\) is the *highest* cell of column \\(c\\) that lies in \\(L\\); any rectangle covering this cell cannot extend above row\u202f1, because rows \\(0\\) do not exist. Hence each such column forces a distinct tile of \\(L\\).  \nTherefore at least \\(k-1\\) left\u2011tiles are necessary.\n\n**Right side.**  \nAnalogously, the cells \\((1,k+1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) belong to \\(R\\).  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n\\) the cell \\((1,c)\\) is the highest cell of column \\(c\\) that lies in \\(R\\); consequently each of those columns forces a distinct tile of \\(R\\).  \nThus at least \\(n-k\\) right\u2011tiles are necessary.\n\nNow delete the first row and the column \\(k\\).  \nWhat remains is an \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) board whose uncovered squares are\n\\((i,\\pi(i))\\) for \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) after renumbering the rows and columns.\nThe conditions of the problem are preserved, so by the induction hypothesis the\nreduced board needs at least \\(2(n-1)-2\\) tiles.\n\nAdding the tiles forced by the first row we obtain a total of at least  \n\n\\[\n(k-1)+(n-k)+\\bigl[2(n-1)-2\\bigr]=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.  This completes the induction and proves (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).\n\n*Tiles covering the region below the diagonal (\\(L\\)).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{vert}}=\\{(r,c):c+1\\le r\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt has size \\((n-c)\\times1\\) and contains exactly the cells of column \\(c\\) that lie\nstrictly below the diagonal.  \nThe \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{vert}}\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together\ncover all of \\(L\\).\n\n*Tiles covering the region above the diagonal (\\(R\\)).*  \nFor each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_r^{\\text{hor}}=\\{(r,c):r+1\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt has size \\(1\\times (n-r)\\) and contains exactly the cells of row \\(r\\) that lie\nstrictly above the diagonal.  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint and together cover all of \\(R\\).\n\nNo tile contains a diagonal cell, the tiles do not overlap, and every non\u2011diagonal\ncell is covered.  The total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower\u2011bound proof shows that\nno admissible placement can use fewer.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles\nfor a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
