und – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary** –  \n   *Left side*: consider the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n   In column \\(c\\) the uncovered squares are absent, so the cells that must be covered are exactly the rows with \\(\\pi(r)>c\\); this set of rows is a suffix of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Any rectangle that contains a cell of column \\(c\\) must be completely contained in that suffix, otherwise it would contain a hole.  \n   Consequently, each rectangle can cover cells of **only one** maximal column value \\(c\\); otherwise its column interval would contain a column \\(c'\\) whose required rows are a *strict* sub‑suffix, forcing the rectangle to miss some cells of column \\(c\\).  \n   Hence at least one rectangle is needed for each column \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\); therefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover all left‑side cells.\n\n   The same argument applied to the right side (using the sets \\(R_c\\)) shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed.\n\n   Thus any tiling satisfying the condition uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n   tiles.\n\n6. **Conclusion** –  \n   The construction gives a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower‑bound argument shows that no tiling can use fewer.  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain the minimum number of tiles\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the rows by \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and the columns by \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square, there is a function  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nThe condition that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square forces \\(\\pi\\) to be a bijection; i.e. \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nHence the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares, \\([n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\), must be covered by axis‑parallel rectangular tiles whose interiors are pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Geometry of the cells that lie left or right of the holes  \n\nFor a fixed permutation \\(\\pi\\) define, for each column \\(c\\),\n\n\\[\nL_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)>c\\},\\qquad   \nR_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_c\\) consists of the cells that are **to the left** of the hole in their row, while \\(R_c\\) consists of the cells that are **to the right** of the hole.\n\n*Observation.*  \nFor a given column \\(c\\) the set of rows that appear in \\(L_c\\) is a suffix of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\): if \\(\\pi(r)>c\\) and \\(r'<r\\) then \\(\\pi(r')\\ge\\pi(r)>c\\) cannot be guaranteed, but the collection \\(\\{r\\mid\\pi(r)>c\\}\\) is precisely the set of rows whose hole lies strictly to the right of column \\(c\\). When the rows are ordered by increasing \\(\\pi(r)\\) (i.e. by the column of the hole), this set is a suffix. An analogous statement holds for \\(R_c\\), which is a prefix of the same ordering.\n\nConsequently, any rectangle that contains a cell of column \\(c\\) and does **not** contain a hole must be completely contained in that suffix (for \\(L_c\\)) or that prefix (for \\(R_c\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nIt suffices to exhibit one permutation for which a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles exists; the minimum over all permutations cannot be larger.\n\nTake the **identity permutation** \\(\\pi(i)=i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left‑hand side.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\