"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Permutation of holes** \u2013 Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n2. **Left\u2011 and right\u2011hand cells** \u2013  \n   For a column \\(c\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)>c\\},\\qquad   \n   R_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L_c\\) consists of the cells that lie **to the left** of the hole in their row, \\(R_c\\) those that lie **to the right**.\n\n3. **Upper bound (construction)** \u2013  \n   Take the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n   *Left side*: for each \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T^{\\text L}_c=\\{(r,c')\\mid r\\ge c+1,\\;1\\le c'\\le c\\},\n   \\]\n\n   of size \\((n-c)\\times c\\).  \n   *Right side*: for each \\(c=2,\\dots ,n\\) place  \n\n   \\[\n   T^{\\text R}_c=\\{(r,c')\\mid r\\le c-1,\\;c\\le c'\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n\n   of size \\((c-1)\\times (n-c+1)\\).  \n   The two families are pairwise disjoint, they cover every square except the \\(n\\) diagonal squares, and no rectangle contains a diagonal square.  \n   The number of rectangles used is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n4. **Lower bound (at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary)**  \n\n   *Left side.*  \n   For each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   r_c=\\max\\{\\,r\\mid \\pi(r)>c\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The cell \\((r_c,c)\\) is the **highest** cell of column \\(c\\) that must be covered (it is left of its row\u2011hole).  \n   The rows \\(r_1,r_2,\\dots ,r_{n-1}\\) are **pairwise distinct**: if \\(c_1<c_2\\) then \\(\\pi(r_{c_2})>c_2>c_1\\), so \\(r_{c_2}\\) belongs to the set defining \\(r_{c_1}\\); maximality forces \\(r_{c_2}\\neq r_{c_1}\\).  \n\n   Consider any rectangle \\(R\\) that covers a cell of the form \\((r_c,c)\\).  \n   Because a rectangle may not contain an uncovered square, all rows of \\(R\\) must belong to the set \\(\\{\\,r\\mid \\pi(r)>c\\,\\}\\); otherwise a row outside this set would place the uncovered square of its row inside \\(R\\).  \n   Hence the **top side** of \\(R\\) is at or above row \\(r_c\\).  \n   If the same rectangle also covered a cell \\((r_{c'},c')\\) with \\(c'\\neq c\\), then its top side would have to be at or above both \\(r_c\\) and \\(r_{c'}\\).  \n   Since the rows \\(r_c\\) are distinct, the rectangle could cover at most one of the cells \\((r_c,c)\\).  \n   Consequently **each column \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) requires a distinct rectangle** to cover its highest left\u2011hand cell.  \n   Therefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for all left\u2011hand cells.\n\n   *Right side.*  \n   An entirely symmetric argument applied to the sets  \n\n   \\[\n   s_c=\\min\\{\\,r\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\,\\},\\qquad c=2,\\dots ,n,\n   \\]\n\n   (the lowest cells that lie to the right of their row\u2011hole) shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for the right\u2011hand cells.\n\n   Adding the two lower bounds we obtain that **any admissible tiling uses at least**  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013 The construction of step\u202f3 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and step\u202f4 proves that no tiling can use fewer.  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) the minimum number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nNumber the rows \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a function  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nThe condition that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square forces \\(\\pi\\) to be a bijection; i.e. \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nHence the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares, \\([n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\), must be covered by axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles whose interiors are pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Cells that lie left or right of the holes  \n\nFor a fixed permutation \\(\\pi\\) define, for each column \\(c\\),\n\n\\[\nL_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)>c\\},\\qquad   \nR_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_c\\) consists of the cells that are **to the left** of the uncovered square in their row; \\(R_c\\) consists of the cells that are **to the right** of the uncovered square in their row.  \nEvery cell that is not in \\(U\\) belongs to exactly one of the sets \\(L_c\\) or \\(R_c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nIt suffices to exhibit one permutation for which a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles exists; the minimum over all permutations cannot be larger.\n\nTake the **identity permutation** \\(\\pi(i)=i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left\u2011hand side.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) consider the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text L}_{c}= \\{(r,c')\\mid r\\ge c+1,\\;1\\le c'\\le c\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts dimensions are \\((n-c)\\times c\\).  \nAll cells of column \\(c\\) that lie left of the diagonal are precisely those with row index \\(r\\ge c+1\\); they are all contained in \\(T^{\\text L}_{c}\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T^{\\text L}_{c}\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) is pairwise disjoint, because each rectangle uses a distinct set of columns.\n\n*Right\u2011hand side.*  \nSymmetrically, for each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text R}_{c}= \\{(r,c')\\mid r\\le c-1,\\;c\\le c'\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nof size \\((c-1)\\times (n-c+1)\\).  \nThese rectangles cover precisely the cells that lie to the right of the diagonal.\n\nTogether the \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) diagonal squares, and no rectangle contains a diagonal square.  \nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nWe prove that **any** permutation \\(\\pi\\) forces the use of at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles to cover the left\u2011hand cells, and similarly at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles for the right\u2011hand cells.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.1  A distinct \u201chighest\u201d left\u2011hand cell for each column  \n\nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nr_c=\\max\\{\\,r\\mid \\pi(r)>c\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set inside the maximum is non\u2011empty because \\(\\pi\\) is a bijection, so there is at least one row whose hole lies strictly to the right of column \\(c\\).  \nThe cell \\((r_c,c)\\) is the **highest** cell of column \\(c\\) that must be covered (it belongs to \\(L_c\\)).\n\n**Claim.** The rows \\(r_1,r_2,\\dots ,r_{n-1}\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n\n*Proof.* Suppose \\(c_1<c_2\\) and \\(r_{c_1}=r_{c_2}=r\\).  \nSince \\(\\pi(r)>c_2\\) we also have \\(\\pi(r)>c_1\\); thus \\(r\\) belongs to the set defining \\(r_{c_1}\\).  \nBut \\(r_{c_1}\\) is the **maximum** of that set, so any element of the set that is larger than \\(r\\) would contradict maximality.  \nBecause \\(\\pi(r)>c_2>c_1\\), the definition of \\(r_{c_2}\\) guarantees that there is **no** row larger than \\(r\\) with \\(\\pi(\\cdot)>c_2\\); however there may be a row larger than \\(r\\) with \\(\\pi(\\cdot)>c_1\\).  \nIf such a larger row existed, it would also belong to the set for \\(c_2\\) (since \\(\\pi(\\cdot)>c_2\\) would then hold), contradicting the maximality of \\(r_{c_2}=r\\).  \nHence no larger row exists for either column, and the only way the maxima could coincide is if the two columns are equal, which they are not.  \u220e\n\nThus we have \\(n-1\\) distinct cells \\((r_c,c)\\), one in each column \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2  Each such cell forces a distinct rectangle  \n\nConsider any rectangle \\(R\\) used in a tiling.  \nBecause a rectangle may not contain an uncovered square, every row that appears in \\(R\\) must belong to the set  \n\n\\[\nS_c=\\{\\,r\\mid \\pi(r)>c\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nfor **every** column \\(c\\) that \\(R\\) occupies; otherwise a row outside \\(S_c\\) would place the uncovered square of that row inside \\(R\\).  \nConsequently, if \\(R\\) contains a cell of column \\(c\\), then all rows of \\(R\\) lie in \\(S_c\\).  \nIn particular the **top side** of \\(R\\) (the smallest row index occurring in \\(R\\)) is at most \\(r_c\\), because \\(r_c\\) is the maximal element of \\(S_c\\).\n\nSuppose a single rectangle \\(R\\) covered two distinct cells \\((r_c,c)\\) and \\((r_{c'},c')\\) with \\(c\\neq c'\\).  \nThen the rows of \\(R\\) would have to belong simultaneously to \\(S_c\\) and to \\(S_{c'}\\); hence they would belong to the intersection \\(S_c\\cap S_{c'}\\).  \nIf, say, \\(c<c'\\), then \\(S_{c'}\\subseteq S_c\\) and the intersection equals \\(S_{c'}\\).  \nTherefore the top side of \\(R\\) would have to be at most \\(r_{c'}\\).  \nBut \\(r_c\\neq r_{c'}\\) (by the claim above), and the cell \\((r_c,c)\\) lies **above** row \\(r_{c'}\\); consequently \\((r_c,c)\\) could not belong to \\(R\\).  \nThus a rectangle cannot contain two of the cells \\((r_c,c)\\) with different columns.\n\nHence each column \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) requires a **different** rectangle to cover its highest left\u2011hand cell.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover all left\u2011hand cells.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.3  The right\u2011hand side  \n\nDefine for each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\n\n\\[\ns_c=\\min\\{\\,r\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nthe **lowest** cell of column \\(c\\) that lies to the right of its row\u2011hole (it belongs to \\(R_c\\)).  \nExactly the same argument as in \u00a7\u00a74.1\u20134.2, applied to the sets  \n\n\\[\nT_c=\\{\\,r\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nshows that the rows \\(s_2,\\dots ,s_n\\) are pairwise distinct and that each column \\(c\\) needs a distinct rectangle to cover its cell \\((s_c,c)\\).  \nConsequently at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for the right\u2011hand cells.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.4  Total lower bound  \n\nAdding the two independent requirements we obtain that any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Determination of the minimum  \n\nThe construction of \u00a73 achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower\u2011bound argument of \u00a74 proves that no tiling can use fewer.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
