"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares** \u2013  \n   Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there is a bijection  \n   \\[\n   \\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad n=2025,\n   \\]\n   such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n2. **Left\u2011 and right\u2011regions** \u2013  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,j):j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \n   R=\\{(i,j):j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n   \\]\n   Every covered cell lies either in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\); a rectangle cannot contain cells from both regions, otherwise it would also contain the uncovered square of that row. Hence each tile is wholly inside \\(L\\) or wholly inside \\(R\\).\n\n3. **Lower bound \\(2n-2\\)** \u2013  \n   *Key Lemma (left side).*  \n   For each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\) consider the **rightmost** cell of that row that belongs to \\(L\\), namely \\((i,\\pi(i)-1)\\).  \n   **Claim.** No rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain two such right\u2011most cells belonging to different rows.  \n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Suppose a rectangle \\(T\\subseteq L\\) contains \\((i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\((j,\\pi(j)-1)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \n   Without loss of generality \\(\\pi(i)\\le \\pi(j)\\).  \n   The right side of \\(T\\) is at column \\(\\ge \\pi(j)-1\\); therefore in row \\(i\\) the rectangle also covers column \\(\\pi(i)\\), which is the uncovered square of that row \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e  \n\n   Consequently each rectangle of \\(L\\) can accommodate **at most one** of the \\(n-1\\) right\u2011most cells (the row for which \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) has none). Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(L\\).\n\n   An analogous argument applied to the **leftmost** cells of the rows of \\(R\\) (the cells \\((i,\\pi(i)+1)\\) for rows with \\(\\pi(i)<n\\)) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(R\\).\n\n   Adding the two estimates gives the universal lower bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2n-2}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound** \u2013  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal.  \n\n   *Tiles covering \\(L\\) (the cells strictly below the diagonal).*  \n   For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_c^{\\text{vert}}=\\{(r,c):c+1\\le r\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. a \\( (n-c)\\times1\\) tile.  \n   The \\(n-1\\) tiles \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{vert}}\\}\\) are disjoint and together cover every cell of \\(L\\).\n\n   *Tiles covering \\(R\\) (the cells strictly above the diagonal).*  \n   For each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_r^{\\text{hor}}=\\{(r,c):r+1\\le c\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. a \\(1\\times (n-r)\\) tile.  \n   These \\(n-1\\) tiles are disjoint, avoid the diagonal, and together cover all of \\(R\\).\n\n   The two families are disjoint, they never contain a diagonal cell, and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal unit square.  \n   The total number of tiles used is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same value; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of rectangular tiles must satisfy\n\n* each tile is axis\u2011parallel with its sides on grid lines,\n* tiles are pairwise interior\u2011disjoint,\n* each unit square is covered by at most one tile,\n* each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains precisely one uncovered square, there exists a function  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nThe condition that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square forces \\(\\pi\\) to be a bijection; hence \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j):j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered cell belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\).  \nIf a rectangle contained cells from both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) in the same row, it would also contain the uncovered cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) of that row, which is forbidden.  \nHence **each tile lies entirely inside \\(L\\) or entirely inside \\(R\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\n#### Lemma 1 (left side).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\) let  \n\n\\[\na_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\n\\]\n\nbe the rightmost cell of that row that belongs to \\(L\\).  \nNo rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain two distinct points among the set \\(\\{a_i:\\pi(i)>1\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\subseteq L\\) contains \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_j\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nWithout loss of generality \\(\\pi(i)\\le\\pi(j)\\).  \nThe right side of \\(T\\) is at column \\(\\ge\\pi(j)-1\\); therefore in row \\(i\\) the rectangle also covers column \\(\\pi(i)\\), which is the uncovered square of that row \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) points of the form \\(a_i\\) (the row for which \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) contributes none).  \nSince each rectangle of \\(L\\) can contain at most one of them, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(L\\).\n\n#### Lemma 2 (right side).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nb_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\n\\]\n\nbe the leftmost cell of that row that belongs to \\(R\\).  \nAn argument symmetric to Lemma\u202f1 shows that no rectangle contained in \\(R\\) can contain two distinct points among the set \\(\\{b_i:\\pi(i)<n\\}\\).  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(R\\).\n\nAdding the two estimates we obtain the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any admissible tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Covering the region below the diagonal (\\(L\\)).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{vert}}=\\{(r,c):c+1\\le r\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\((n-c)\\times1\\) tile.  \nThe \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{vert}}\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover every cell of \\(L\\).\n\n*Covering the region above the diagonal (\\(R\\)).*  \nFor each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_r^{\\text{hor}}=\\{(r,c):r+1\\le c\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-r)\\) tile.  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint, avoid the diagonal, and together cover all of \\(R\\).\n\nThe two families do not intersect, they never contain a diagonal cell, and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal unit square.  \nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and Lemma\u202f1\u202f+\u202fLemma\u202f2 prove that no admissible tiling can use fewer.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
